        {
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "heading": "Color adjectives agree",
              "body": "Colors change to match the noun. Feminine usually adds -e: noir → noire, bleu → bleue, vert → verte. \"Blanc\" becomes \"blanche\". \"Rouge\" already ends in -e, so it never changes. Plural adds -s: des chaussures noires.",
              "examples": [
                { "french": "un pantalon noir", "english": "black trousers", "note": "masculine" },
                { "french": "une robe noire", "english": "a black dress", "note": "feminine: + e" },
                { "french": "un manteau blanc, une chemise blanche", "english": "a white coat, a white shirt" },
                { "french": "des chaussures bleues", "english": "blue shoes", "note": "feminine plural: + es" },
                { "french": "un pull vert, une jupe verte", "english": "a green sweater, a green skirt", "note": "you hear the t in \"verte\"" },
                { "french": "un tee-shirt jaune, une robe jaune", "english": "a yellow t-shirt, a yellow dress", "note": "\"jaune\" already ends in -e, so it never changes" }
              ],
              "tip": "For many colors, the extra -e is silent: \"bleu\" and \"bleue\" sound the same. But you hear it in \"vert / verte\" and \"blanc / blanche\"."
            }
          ]
        },
